{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>My ordinances you shall do, etc.</b> It is difficult: since He is speaking only about the violation of the forbidden sexual relations, what are the statutes and ordinances which HaShem our Elohim commanded us? Also, what is His intention in saying <b>to walk in them</b>? Also, one needs to know why He repeated a second time, saying ״אני ה׳ אלהיכם״?" "<b>And it appears</b> to explain according to what I wrote nearby, that thought about forbidden relations intensifies the desire and diminishes the restraint of the will. The verse was concerned that a person might say to distance himself entirely from every detail of this matter and nullify the commandment of procreation and the commandment of levirate marriage and similar things, and he would be free from this matter completely — as our Sages, may their memory be for blessing, said (Sukkah 52b): a small organ man has; starve it and it is satisfied, satisfy it and it is hungry. Therefore HaShem commanded and said <b>My ordinances you shall do</b> — these being matters of the very kind that I forbade to you, I obligate you to perform [i.e., permitted relations within marriage]; and even so, <b>My statutes you shall keep</b> — these being the forbidden relations that He forbade." "Or it may mean that through the very act of guarding against forbidden relations, they will find preparedness in their souls to fulfill positive commandments, as He said <b>My ordinances you shall do</b>, and the observance of all negative commandments, as He said <b>My statutes you shall keep</b>. This is unlike one who abhors his deeds in that way [of sin], whose soul sickens with the diseases of impurity and he will not fulfill even one of them, for the impurity of this sin casts impurity upon all the limbs and powers of man. And since this is so, he will not refrain from any of the negative commandments, for in every limb his impurity resides; and the same applies to positive commandments — even if he occasionally performs some commandment, it is not a [valid] commandment, for the Creator, blessed be His name, finds no favor in it, as He said (Psalms 50:16): ״ולרשע אמר אלהים מה לך לספר חקי״ etc. — for HaShem has no desire in them except in a commandment performed in holiness." "And His saying <b>to walk in them</b> intends to say: regarding our path with a kind of stringency, for when a person is pure and holy, free of the abomination, it will be born into his nature to fulfill the commandments always, as He said (Psalms 119:59) ״חשבתי דרכי ואשיבה רגלי אל עדותיך״, and our Sages, may their memory be for blessing, expounded (Leviticus Rabbah 35:1) that David's feet would walk of their own accord to the house of study, by force of habit and chosen desire. Therefore He said <b>to walk in them</b> — meaning, of his own accord he will walk in them by his nature, like one doing things that nature does, because his core and his entire being stand in a pure place." "<b>Further</b>, His saying <b>to walk in them</b> may intend, in the manner of what the prophet said (Ezekiel 33:12): ״צדקת הצדיק לא תזכר ביום שובו״ etc. — that if one was righteous all his days and sins at the end, he has lost his merit and it will not accompany him on the day he journeys to the supernal world. Therefore HaShem commanded and said ״ללכת בהם״ — meaning, that he persist in good deeds and observance of the statutes, so that they may accompany him on the day he sets out on the path of all the earth." "<b>Further,</b> it intends that a person should not hope for the reward of mitzvot in this world, along the lines of what they said: ״Today to do them and tomorrow to receive their reward״ (Eiruvin 22a) — so that whatever he does, he should not choose to eat his world in his lifetime, but rather his righteousness should walk before him to gaze upon the pleasantness of HaShem." "<b>Further,</b> it intends along the lines of what Scripture says: ״For a mitzvah is a lamp and Torah is light״ (Proverbs 6:23), for the supernal world is distant in its course, and when a person's soul ascends from this world to the supernal world, if he is wicked — the way of the wicked is as darkness. And when a person walks fittingly, the mitzvot and Torah that are in his hand illuminate for him, as it says: ״A lamp to my feet is Your word and a light to my path״ (Psalms 119:105). For the intention is that there is something in a lamp that is not in light, and there is something in light that is not in a lamp: the lamp has a great advantage over light when it is close, and therefore our Rabbis said (Pesachim 2a) that the search for leaven must be done at night by the light of a lamp, for a lamp is fine for searching, to insert it into holes and crevices, which is not so with light. And light has a great advantage over a lamp in that one sees far with it, which is not so with a lamp. Therefore David said that the mitzvot of HaShem have in their illumination both aspects mentioned: the aspect of the lamp for what is close — this is what he said, ״A lamp to my feet,״ which is close by — and the aspect of light for what is far — this is what he said, ״and a light to my path.״ And this is what it says here: <b>to walk in them</b> — meaning, in their illumination he walks; for if not, he walks in darkness and does not know by what the Merciful One may cause him to stumble, may the Compassionate One spare us." "<b>Further,</b> it intends along the lines of what the divine Tanna Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai, peace be upon him, said (Zohar 1:170a): that the 248 limbs of a person and his 365 sinews correspond to positive commandments and negative commandments, and when a person performs a mitzvah with that limb through which the mitzvah is performed, the name YHWH, blessed be He, dwells upon it — for the mitzvah is the letters of the name YHWH; half is revealed, and this is the sod of the world of revelation (alma d'itgalya), and half is hidden, the sod of the world of concealment (alma d'itkasya), for the letters מ״צ by the א״ת ב״ש substitution are י״ה. And it is a known matter that the Holy One, blessed be He — He is His Name, and His Name is He, in the sod of what is said (Zechariah 14:9): ״HaShem is one and His Name is one.״ You have been shown to know that in the performance of the mitzvah a person becomes a chariot for the Shekhinah, and HaShem walks within him; and this is what it says: <b>to walk in them</b>. And it returned and explained who it is that walks in them, and said: <b>I am HaShem</b> — as if it said: לָלֶכֶת — I, HaShem, in them; and this is the sod of (Exodus 25:8): ״וְשָׁכַנְתִּי בְּתוֹכָם.״ And according to this, the verse intends ״in them״ according to its plain sense, for in the performance of the mitzvah the preparation is made for Him to walk in it; and this is the sod of what it says (Leviticus 26:12): ״וְהִתְהַלַּכְתִּי בְּתוֹכְכֶם,״ (Deuteronomy 4:4): ״וְאַתֶּם הַדְּבֵקִים בַּה׳ אֱלֹהֵיכֶם״:"], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:58:33.725316997Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}